before:
Worked example:

```javascript
function parseBody(text) {
  try { return {ok:true,value:JSON.parse(text)}; }
  catch { return {ok:false}; }
}
console.log(parseBody("{\"minutes\":5}"));
```
The returned value is {"ok":true,"value":{"minutes":5}}.

after:
```javascript
function parseBody(text) {
  try { return {ok:true,value:JSON.parse(text)}; }
  catch { return {ok:false}; }
}
console.log(parseBody("{\"minutes\":5}"));
```
The result has ok set to true and value set to an object whose minutes is 5. The backslashes in the input let quotation marks appear inside a JavaScript string.
